{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded the account for the partition receipt 91832b58570e4cc7842cfff4d04836e0 (51 events, exit 0) and closed the last boundary the account had listed as a limitation: comparisons 0, 1 and 2 are violated with one valid witness at parsed index 48 — after the partition the restarted node led on its one-voter reconstruction while its two peers led on the committed three-voter configuration, both in the same term, both committing a uniquely named entry successfully, and neither side's store held the other's entry, so the cluster held two leaders and two committed histories at once — while the flag-disabled control holds at parsed index 50 with a single leader and the restarted node refused with \\\"node is not the leader\\\". I added a fact for that split brain, cited the receipt from the support-arithmetic fact that quorumSize and the commitment read the node's own configuration, withdrew the limitation that the partition case was untested, and left the discriminator holding only store and fault-model boundaries: disk-backed stores, crash and restart timing, partition healing with client traffic, and repeated or longer runs. The question stays explained with the defect claim asserted, so the account now covers every consequence identified in this run under its own receipts, from bounded non-progress through minority commits, a discarded acknowledged entry, votes and leadership granted without entitlement, a rewritten membership, and finally two leaders in one term under a partition. This was the turn's only write.\"}"}}
